NAME
  sh - Freax command interpreter

SYNOPSIS
  sh
  sh FILE [ARG...]
  sh -c COMMAND [NAME [ARG...]]

DESCRIPTION
  sh runs interactive commands or shell scripts. Script mode does not load
  /etc/profile.lua or print the login banner.

  FILE sets $0 to its resolved path. ARG values become $1, $2, and so on.
  With -c, NAME becomes $0; it defaults to sh.

EXPANSION
  Environment variables use $NAME or ${NAME}. Single quotes suppress
  expansion. $? is the last exit status. $# is the argument count. $0 and
  $1 through $9 select positional parameters; use ${10} for larger indexes.
  $* joins all parameters. "$@" as a complete word preserves each parameter
  as one argument. $! is the process id of the last background job.

  '*' and '?' perform pathname expansion. '~' expands to $HOME. Backticks
  perform command substitution and remove trailing newlines.

COMMANDS
  Commands may be joined with ';', '&&', or '||'. '!' negates status.
  Pipelines use '|'. Redirects include '<', '>', '>>', '2>', and '2>&1'.
  A trailing '&' runs the command in the background.

  Every stage of a pipeline shares one process group, so a signal sent to the
  group (Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z, kill) affects the whole pipeline.

  Aliases apply at command positions. Builtins include cd, export, unset,
  alias, unalias, source, jobs, fg, bg, wait, kill, exit, and logout.

LINE EDITING KEYS
  Enter          submit the line
  Backspace      delete the character before the cursor
  Delete         delete the character under the cursor
  Left, Right    move the cursor
  Home, End      move to start or end of the line
  Up, Down       recall earlier or newer history entries
  Tab            complete command, alias, or path
  Ctrl+C         cancel the current line, or interrupt the foreground job
  Ctrl+D         end of input: with text, delete the character under the
                 cursor; on an empty line, leave the shell
  Ctrl+Z         suspend the foreground job

  Ctrl+D on an empty line ends the shell the same way logout does, returning
  the user to the login prompt.

JOB CONTROL
  COMMAND &        run COMMAND in the background as job [n]
  jobs             list background jobs and their state
  fg [%JOB]        resume a stopped job in the foreground and wait for it
  bg [%JOB]        resume a stopped job in the background
  wait [%JOB]      wait for a job, or for every job when no argument is given
  kill %JOB        send a signal to a job's process group

  %JOB is %n (job number), %+ (most recent job), %- (previous job), or %prefix
  (first job whose command starts with prefix).

  Ctrl+C interrupts the foreground job. Ctrl+Z suspends it so bg or fg can
  continue it. A background job's standard input is closed, so it cannot steal
  input from the prompt. Suspended jobs report status 148.

  Builtins cannot be backgrounded: they have no process of their own and no
  subshell is created yet. Run them through `sh -c` when a separate process is
  required.

COMPOUND COMMANDS
  Compound keywords must appear at line boundaries.

    if COMMAND; then
      COMMANDS
    else
      COMMANDS
    fi

    for NAME in WORDS; do
      COMMANDS
    done

    for NAME; do
      COMMANDS
    done

  The last form iterates over positional parameters. Compound commands may
  nest up to 15 levels.

SOURCE
  source FILE [ARG...]
  . FILE [ARG...]

  Source runs FILE in the current shell, so aliases, environment changes,
  and working-directory changes remain. Supplied arguments temporarily
  replace positional parameters; without arguments, current parameters are
  inherited. Source nesting is limited to 10 levels.

LIMITATIONS
  while/until, functions, case, traps, here-documents, arithmetic expansion,
  and general prefix/suffix use around "$@" are not implemented yet. Builtins
  cannot be pipeline stages or run in the background. The terminal supports one
  foreground job at a time; nested shells do not take over the terminal.

EXAMPLES
  sh /tmp/build.sh input "two words"
  sh -c 'ls /bin && echo ready'
  sleep 300 &
  jobs
  fg %1
  kill %1
